Silo patch 0036 — the DualSense's own lights when a game gets the pad

Why: Steam for macOS lights a DualSense in its own colour (the user's Steam pad colour) when it opens it, and with
Steam Input off for a game — Wine reading the pad itself, through patches 0019-0035 — that colour stayed, or
changed to the game's only when a race with the Bluetooth writer happened to go the game's way (TEKKEN 8,
2026-11-27). Steam's colour means "Steam Input drives this pad" to the user, which it doesn't then. So when Wine
starts a DualSense for a game, the light bar is taken over from the firmware (light out, as Linux's
hid-playstation does) and set blue (0, 0, 128, hid-playstation's PS5-like default), with the player indicator on
player 1; a colour or player indicator the game writes replaces those. Steam writes its colour again each time it
activates the pad for an app (2 s after the game started, and on every return to Steam), so the lights are
written again every 2 s, from the input path the way the trigger settings are followed, through the game's own
output path (the Bluetooth writer, or report 0x02 on a cable). Written once only, Steam won within 2 s. With Steam Input on the pad is hidden from
Wine (Silo's Hidraw=0) and never started, so Steam's colour stays.

